!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/link/valid/an-instantiation-composes-like-a-record-expected.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/link-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §4.3, [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.8, [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10, [TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2"
  description: """
    Both operator families consume values a type states rules for, and the test is on the value and on nothing else
    (§4.3): `bx` closes to a `!meta.record` carrying `item`, so `bx & { extra: text }` merges its fields as it would
    any record's. What an author wrote cannot decide it: §8.2 keys identity on the type and not on how it was spelled,
    so composing with a name for an application admits exactly what composing with the application admits. An
    application that constructs something with no fields, `meta.array<text>`, is refused as an operand. The
    `subtypes` claim states the admission from outside: composition grants IS-A per parent (§5.8), so `bx` indexes
    `sub`, which a refusal would leave empty.
    """
  valid: {
    binds: [box bx sub]
    subtypes: [
      { name: bx  subtypes: [sub] }
    ]
  }
}
